::Q1::What is the relationship among AI, ML, and GenAI described in the module?
{ 
=ML is a subset of AI, and GenAI is a more specific category within ML
~The three terms describe unrelated technologies
~AI is a subset of GenAI, which is a subset of ML
~GenAI contains AI but not ML
}

::Q2::How does a typical machine learning model learn? 
{
~It stores only the final prediction
~It never uses data
=It learns patterns from examples in training data
~It manually receives every possible rule from a user
}

::Q3::Which task is an example of generative AI?
{ 
~Counting rows with a SQL query only
~Selecting a fixed spam/not-spam label only
~Checking whether a password matches a policy
=Generating a natural-language explanation of code
}

::Q4::Which example best represents traditional machine learning rather than generative AI?
{
=Classifying an email as spam or not spam
~Creating an image from a description
~Generating source code
~Writing a new paragraph of text
}

::Q5::What does the nested relationship imply?
{
~Every AI system must be generative
~Generative AI is unrelated to machine learning
~Every ML system generates content
=Every generative AI system described here is within the broader machine-learning and AI categories
}